# Reflexion: an outreach email that critiques and revises itself

The [[analyst]] agent drafts an outreach email in one shot. One shot is rarely
the best shot. A **Reflexion** agent closes the loop: it **generates** a draft,
**critiques** it against an explicit rubric, **revises** in light of the
critique, and repeats — keeping the whole trace as **memory** so each pass can
see what the last pass already tried and complained about.

The shape is three roles in a cycle:

- **generate** — produce (or rewrite) the draft from the brief plus the running
  memory of prior critiques.
- **critique** — score the draft against a rubric and write down concrete,
  actionable gripes. This is a self-evaluation step, not a human review gate.
- **revise** — fold the critique back into the next generate call.

Memory is **the trace itself**: a list of `%Reflection{}` values, one per pass.
Unlike the [[researcher]] loop (whose memory is verified findings) or the
[[coder]] agent (whose memory is the ReAct scratchpad of tool calls), Reflexion's
memory is a chain of *self-judgments*. The agent's own past verdicts are the
context that makes the next draft better.

Like every brain in this app, the loop runs **server** side — native Elixir on
the BEAM, not wasm. The model calls go out through the Dock with a `net` grant;
the loop, the memory, and the typed state all live in real OTP. The browser only
sees the final approved draft.

## The typed state

A draft is a struct, and so is a single pass of reflection. No DSL, no typespec —
the field types are **inferred from the defaults**. The `:lead` field carries a
canonical [[score]] `%Lead{}`, because the email is *about* a specific lead and
the copy leans on its enriched facts.

agent :reflexion do
  defmodule Draft do
    defstruct lead: nil, subject: "", body: "", pass: 0
  end

  # One self-judgment. `verdict` is an atom enum; `score` is the rubric total
  # out of 100; `gripes` are the concrete fixes the next pass must apply.
  defmodule Reflection do
    defstruct pass: 0, score: 0, verdict: :reject, gripes: []
  end

  @verdicts [:accept, :revise, :reject]

  # The rubric the critic scores against. Each weighted line is checked
  # independently so the gripes can point at the exact failing dimension.
  @rubric [
    {:personalized, 30, "names the lead's domain and a fact about its size"},
    {:concrete_value, 30, "states one specific outcome, not a generic pitch"},
    {:short, 20, "under 90 words, one ask, no filler"},
    {:clear_cta, 20, "ends with a single, low-friction next step"}
  ]
end

## Generate

The generator takes the brief, the lead, and the **memory so far**. On pass 0 the
memory is empty and it writes from scratch; on later passes it is handed the most
recent `%Reflection{}` and told to fix exactly those gripes. The prompt is
assembled from the running trace, which is what makes this Reflexion and not a
plain retry.

agent :reflexion do
  alias Sales.Dock

  @model "minimax/minimax-m3"

  def generate(%Lead{} = lead, brief, memory) do
    prompt =
      case memory do
        [] ->
          """
          Write a cold outreach email to #{lead.name} (#{lead.domain}).
          Context: ~#{lead.employees} employees, ~$#{lead.revenue} revenue.
          Brief: #{brief}
          Return a subject line and a body under 90 words.
          """

        [%Reflection{gripes: gripes, score: score} | _] ->
          """
          Your previous draft scored #{score}/100 and was rejected.
          Rewrite it. Fix every one of these problems, change nothing else:
          #{Enum.map_join(gripes, "\n", &("- " <> &1))}

          Lead: #{lead.name} (#{lead.domain}), ~#{lead.employees} employees.
          Brief: #{brief}
          """
      end

    %{"subject" => subject, "body" => body} =
      Dock.complete(@model, prompt, grant: [net: "api.crm.com"], format: :json)

    %Draft{lead: lead, subject: subject, body: body, pass: length(memory)}
  end
end

## Critique

The critic is the heart of the technique: the agent grades its **own** work. It
runs each rubric line, sums the weighted score, and turns every failing line into
a gripe string the next `generate/3` can act on. The verdict is an atom from
`@verdicts` — `:accept` when the score clears the bar, `:revise` otherwise.

The critique is a model call too, but a *narrow* one: each rubric line is a
yes/no judgment with a one-line reason, which keeps the self-evaluation honest
instead of letting the model rate its own essay a vague "8/10".

agent :reflexion do
  @pass_mark 85

  def critique(%Draft{} = draft) do
    judgments =
      Enum.map(@rubric, fn {dim, weight, requirement} ->
        prompt = """
        Email subject: #{draft.subject}
        Email body: #{draft.body}

        Does this email satisfy: "#{requirement}"?
        Answer strictly as {"pass": true|false, "reason": "..."}.
        """

        %{"pass" => pass?, "reason" => reason} =
          Dock.complete(@model, prompt, grant: [net: "api.crm.com"], format: :json)

        {dim, weight, pass?, reason}
      end)

    score =
      Enum.reduce(judgments, 0, fn
        {_dim, weight, true, _reason}, acc -> acc + weight
        {_dim, _weight, false, _reason}, acc -> acc
      end)

    gripes =
      for {_dim, _weight, false, reason} <- judgments, do: reason

    verdict = if score >= @pass_mark, do: :accept, else: :revise

    %Reflection{pass: draft.pass, score: score, verdict: verdict, gripes: gripes}
  end
end

## The loop

`refine/3` ties the three roles together. It generates, critiques, **prepends**
the reflection to the memory list (newest first, so `generate/3` reads
`[latest | _]`), and recurses until the critic says `:accept` or the pass budget
runs out. The accumulated `memory` is the return value alongside the winning
draft — the trace is a first-class output, not a side effect.

There is no iteration cap baked in as a quality gate; `max_passes` is a plain
**safety net** so a stubborn brief can't spin forever. The loop stops when the
work is good, not when a counter expires.

agent :reflexion do
  def refine(%Lead{} = lead, brief, max_passes \\ 4) do
    do_refine(lead, brief, [], max_passes)
  end

  defp do_refine(_lead, _brief, [%Reflection{} = last | _] = memory, 0) do
    {:exhausted, last, Enum.reverse(memory)}
  end

  defp do_refine(lead, brief, memory, passes_left) do
    draft = generate(lead, brief, memory)
    reflection = critique(draft)
    memory = [reflection | memory]

    case reflection.verdict do
      :accept ->
        {:ok, draft, Enum.reverse(memory)}

      _ ->
        do_refine(lead, brief, memory, passes_left - 1)
    end
  end
end

A run over a low-signal lead might look like this — three passes, each fixing the
prior critique, score climbing until it clears `@pass_mark`:

agent :reflexion do
  def demo do
    lead = %Lead{
      name: "Globex",
      domain: "globex.io",
      revenue: 12_000,
      employees: 8,
      score: 41,
      status: :enriched
    }

    case refine(lead, "intro our pipeline tool, book a 15-min call", 4) do
      {:ok, draft, trace} ->
        # trace = [%Reflection{pass: 0, score: 55, verdict: :revise, ...},
        #          %Reflection{pass: 1, score: 75, verdict: :revise, ...},
        #          %Reflection{pass: 2, score: 90, verdict: :accept, gripes: []}]
        {draft.subject, draft.body, length(trace)}

      {:exhausted, last, _trace} ->
        {:gave_up, last.score, last.gripes}
    end
  end
end

## Where it sits

Reflexion is the quality stage between a raw [[analyst]] draft and the outreach
that actually gets sent. The brief comes from the work tracked on the board; the
lead facts come from [[enrich]] and [[score]]; the approved draft flows back out
to a `:contacted` status update.

Because the whole trace is typed `%Reflection{}` data, it is queryable like any
other content — `work why` can show *why* a given email reads the way it does by
walking the chain of self-critiques that shaped it. The agent's reasoning is not
hidden in a log; it is part of the document.
